<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<gretl-functions>
<gretl-function-package name="cffilt" needs-time-series-data="true" minver="2023c" lives-in-subdir="true">
<author email="r.lucchetti@univpm.it">Riccardo Jack Lucchetti</author>
<version>2.2</version>
<date>2024-03-19</date>
<description>Christiano-Fitzgerald asymmetric filter</description>
<tags>C22</tags>
<label>Christiano-Fitzgerald</label>
<menu-attachment>MAINWIN/Variable/Filter</menu-attachment>
<help filename="cffilt.md">
This package filters a time series by means of the Christiano-
Fitzgerald filter (see Christiano &amp; Fitzgerald (2003), &quot;The Band Pass
Filter&quot;, International Economic Review, 44-2). The code used here is
an adaptation from the original Matlab code by Terry Fitzgerald, as
found at https://www.frbatlanta.org/cqer/research/bpf.aspx

# Functions provided

This package provides two functions, cffilt() and cffilt_list(). The
former filters a single series, and returns a series; the latter
filters a named list, and returns a new list. For example

`y = cffilt(x)`

Both functions take the following optional arguments:

2. pl, an optional scalar for the minimum period (minimum = 2)
3. pu, an optional scalar for the maximum period (minimum = 3)
4. an optional Boolean for the unit root assumption (default = yes)
5. an optional Boolean for drift removal (default = yes)

With monthly, quarterly or yearly data, parameters 2 and 3 may be omitted. In this case, they will
default to

pl = 2 (for yearly data), 6 (quarterly), 18 (monthly)

pu = 8 (for yearly data), 32 (quarterly), 96 (monthly)

With other periodicities, arguments 2 and 3 must be supplied explicitly.

**Warning**:  in versions up to 2.0 the two functions had an extra
argument, now suppressed. You may want to revise your scripts in the
light of this change.


# Changelog:

## 2.1 -&gt; 2.2:

Improvements to the the GUI hook (thanks to A. Cottrell). Add the
&quot;artificial&quot; example script. 

## 2.0 -&gt; 2.1:

Drop the &quot;auto-limits&quot; Boolean switch from the function
arguments. Switch to markdown for this file. Also, I modified the
&quot;replication.inp&quot; example script to use the newly-introduced gridplot
command instead of the multiplot function and avoid a dependency on
another package.

	     
## 1.01 -&gt; 2.0: 

the old function cffilt() is now called cffilt_list(); cffilt() now
operates on a single series. Introduce automatic min and max periods
of oscillation. Beef up the help text a little. Switch internally to
more modern syntax.
	     
## 1.0 -&gt; 1.01: 

replace = with == in Boolean expressions; revise the sample script
</help>
<gui-help filename="cffilt_gui.md">
The Christiano-Fitzgerald filter is designed for use with macroeconomic
data observed at one of the usual frequencies: annual, quarterly or
monthly. In that case the default values for the minimum and maximum
periods (the limits of the pass band) should be appropriate to isolate
business cycle frequencies, though you can adjust them if you wish.

If you're calling this function with data of a different frequency (say,
daily, weekly or hourly), the default values for the minimum and maximum
periods are purely notional. Hopefully, you will have some idea of what
you actually want.
</gui-help>
<data-files count="1">
examples </data-files>
<gretl-function name="cffilt" type="series">
 <params count="5">
  <param name="x" type="series">
<description>Input variable</description>
  </param>
  <param name="pl" type="scalar" min="2" default="NA">
<description>Minimum period</description>
  </param>
  <param name="pu" type="scalar" min="3" default="NA">
<description>Maximum period</description>
  </param>
  <param name="unitroot" type="bool" default="1">
<description>Unit root assumed?</description>
  </param>
  <param name="drift" type="bool" default="1">
<description>Drift removal?</description>
  </param>
 </params>
<code>err = autolimits(&amp;pl, &amp;pu)
if err
  series ret = NA
else
  list tmp = cffilt_list(x, pl, pu, unitroot, drift)
  series ret = tmp[1]
  string ds = sprintf(&quot;Filtered %s: Christiano-Fitzgerald, freq %d to %d&quot;, argname(x), pl, pu)
  setinfo ret --description=&quot;@ds&quot;
endif
return ret
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="cffilt_list" type="list">
 <params count="5">
  <param name="L" type="list">
<description>Input variable(s)</description>
  </param>
  <param name="pl" type="scalar" min="2" default="NA">
<description>Minimum period</description>
  </param>
  <param name="pu" type="scalar" min="3" default="NA">
<description>Maximum period</description>
  </param>
  <param name="unitroot" type="bool" default="1">
<description>Unit root assumed</description>
  </param>
  <param name="drift" type="bool" default="1">
<description>Drift removal</description>
  </param>
 </params>
<code>#	Required Inputs:
#  	X		- data matrix (T x n) with variables in columns
#	pl		- minimum period of oscillation of desired component
#	pu		- maximum period of oscillation of desired component (2&lt;=pl&lt;pu&lt;infinity).
#
#   Optional inputs:
#   unitroot - 1 if unitroot is assumed
#   drift - 1 for aurtmatic drift removal
#
#   Output:
#	an n-element list containing the filtered data

list ret = empty
err = autolimits(&amp;pl, &amp;pu)

if err
  return ret
endif

# --- find a common sample first -------------------------------------
scalar t1 = $t1
scalar t2 = $t2
loop foreach i L
  scalar tx1 = firstobs(L.$i)
  t1 = xmax(t1, tx1)
  scalar tx2 = lastobs(L.$i)
  t2 = xmin(t2, tx2)
endloop
smpl t1 t2

# --- set up some stuff used later -----------------------------------
matrix X = {L}
strings names = varnames(L)
scalar b = 2*$pi/pl
scalar a = 2*$pi/pu
scalar T = rows(X)
scalar nvars = cols(X)
matrix Y = {}
if T &lt; 5
  printf &quot;(bpass): warning! T &lt; 5\n&quot;
endif

# --- compute &quot;ideal&quot; Bs ---------------------------------------------

matrix j = seq(1,2*T)'
matrix B = (b-a)/$pi | (sin(j.*b) - sin(j.*a)) ./ (j*$pi)
#    compute R using closed form integral solution
matrix cd = 0 | cum(2 * $pi * B[1:T-1])
matrix R = $pi * B[1] - cd

#----------------------------------
#  asymmetric filter
#----------------------------------

matrix AA = zeros(T,T)
loop i = 1 .. T
  AA[i,i:T] = B[1:T-i+1]'
  if unitroot
    AA[i,T] = R[T+1-i]/(2*$pi)
  endif
endloop
AA[1,1] = AA[T,T]
#  Use symmetry to construct bottom 'half' of AA

B = mshape(mreverse(vec(AA)), T, T)
B[diag] = 0
AA += B

#======================================================================
#
#  check that sum of all filters equal 0 if assuming unit root
#
if unitroot
  scalar tst = maxc(abs(sumr(AA)))
  if tst &gt; 1.e-09
    printf &quot;warning: Bhat does not sum to 0 (sum bhat = %g)\n&quot;, tst
  endif
endif
#
#======================================================================
#
#	compute filtered time series using selected filter matrix AA

Y = drift ? AA * undrift(X) : AA * X

k = 1
loop foreach i L
  string nm = sprintf(&quot;cf_%s&quot;, names[k])
  string ds = sprintf(&quot;Filtered %s: Christiano-Fitzgerald, freq %d to %d&quot;, names[k], pl, pu)
  ret += genseries(nm, Y[,k++])
  setinfo @nm --description=&quot;@ds&quot;
endloop
return ret
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="cffilt_GUI" type="series" no-print="1" pkg-role="gui-main">
 <params count="5">
  <param name="x" type="series">
<description>Input variable</description>
  </param>
  <param name="pl" type="int" min="2" default="2">
<description>Minimum period</description>
  </param>
  <param name="pu" type="int" min="3" default="3">
<description>Maximum period</description>
  </param>
  <param name="unitroot" type="bool" default="1">
<description>Unit root assumed?</description>
  </param>
  <param name="drift" type="bool" default="1">
<description>Drift removal?</description>
  </param>
 </params>
<code>err = autolimits(&amp;pl, &amp;pu)

if err
  series ret = NA
else
  series ret = cffilt(x, pl, pu, unitroot, drift)
  plot ret
    options time-series with-lines
    literal unset ylabel
    printf &quot;set title 'Christiano-Fitzgerald component of %s at frequency %d to %d&quot;, argname(x), pl, pu
  end plot --output=display
endif

return ret
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="undrift" type="matrix" private="1">
 <params count="1">
  <param name="X" type="matrix" const="true"/>
 </params>
<code>#
#  This function removes the drift or a linear time trend
#  from a time series using the formula
#
#			drift = (x(T) - x(1)) / (T-1).
#
#  Input:  x - data matrix x where columns represent different
#          variables, x is (T x # variables).
#  Output: xun - data matrix same size as x with a different
#          drift/trend removed from each variable.
#
scalar T = rows(X)
matrix d = (X[T,] - X[1,])./ (T-1)
return X .- (seq(0,T-1)' .* d)
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="autolimits" type="scalar" private="1">
 <params count="2">
  <param name="pl" type="scalarref"/>
  <param name="pu" type="scalarref"/>
 </params>
<code>### here we try to set reasonable defaults for
### the frequency bounds in the most common cases:
### annual, quarterly, monthly

err = 0
if missing(pl)
  if $pd == 1
    pl = 2
  elif $pd == 4
    pl = 6
  elif $pd == 12
    pl = 18
  endif
endif

if missing(pu)
  if $pd == 1
    pu = 8
  elif $pd == 4
    pu = 32
  elif $pd == 12
    pu = 96
  endif
endif

if missing(pl) || missing(pu)
  printf &quot; auto bandwidth selection is unavailable with the current periodicity\n&quot;
  err = 1
endif

return err
</code>
</gretl-function>
<gretl-function name="UImaker" type="bundle" private="1" pkg-role="ui-maker">
<code>bundle GUIb

if $pd == 1 || $pd == 4 || $pd == 12
  ldefstr = &quot;ceil(1.5*$pd)&quot;
  udefstr = &quot;8*$pd&quot;
  GUIb.pl = _(default=ldefstr)
  GUIb.pu = _(default=udefstr)
endif

return GUIb
</code>
</gretl-function>
<sample-script>
# sample function call
clear 
set verbose off
include cffilt.gfn

# fetch some macro data from St Louis Fed archive 
open fedstl.bin
data gdpc1
data payems
data indpro
series y = ln(gdpc1)

###
### simple usage: on a single series, with default parameters
###

# perform Baxter-King too, for comparison 
series bk_y = bkfilt(y)
setinfo bk_y --description=&quot;Filtered y: Baxter-King, frequency 6 to 32&quot;

# perform the CF filtering
series cf_y = cffilt(y)

# plot the two filtered series
gnuplot bk_y cf_y --time-series --with-lines --output=display

###
### bulk usage: on a named list, with user-specified parameters
###

list X = gdpc1 indpro payems
list L = logs(X)
list CF = cffilt_list(L, 8, 48)
scatters CF --output=display
</sample-script>
</gretl-function-package>
</gretl-functions>
